**`PAYMENTS_SETTLEMENT_MODE`** is the configuration value that controls how `chargeOrder()` writes ledger rows. In `legacy` mode it writes one row per order; in `split` mode it writes two. Release `v412` changed it from `legacy` to `split`. The release job published that config before replacing any instances, and `v411`'s `assertSettlementMode()` throws on values it doesn't recognise, so every checkout on the `v411` instances failed. That was the trigger at 14:02.

**Migration `0042_add_settlement_currency`** is the second step of the `v412` release job, and it ran at 14:05. In one transaction it:

```sql
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ADD COLUMN settlement_currency char(3);
UPDATE ledger_entries SET settlement_currency = 'USD';
ALTER TABLE ledger_entries ALTER COLUMN settlement_currency SET NOT NULL;
```

The column has no default and is `NOT NULL`, so every insert has to supply it. `v411` code never writes that column. As a result, reverting the config to `legacy` and rolling back to `v411` both still failed, now on a not-null violation instead of the mode assertion. The fix was to roll forward to `v412`, which writes the column in both modes.
